"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/733076f6-90f6-4fe5-9667-63ee7a945dca","id":"733076f6-90f6-4fe5-9667-63ee7a945dca","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-30T10:55:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/104914b4-2cae-4db2-97c5-13123c31b2ac","id":"104914b4-2cae-4db2-97c5-13123c31b2ac","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-30T08:33:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/e614c85f-d5ff-459a-adc6-fef2e3650fb6","id":"e614c85f-d5ff-459a-adc6-fef2e3650fb6","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-30T04:00:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/ea8236c2-f9e1-4093-b8ac-2fb6c98e35c4","id":"ea8236c2-f9e1-4093-b8ac-2fb6c98e35c4","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-30T01:30:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/eebafe31-3888-4ca5-846d-5b3ada5e8888","id":"eebafe31-3888-4ca5-846d-5b3ada5e8888","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T23:27:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/5a73fea9-8bab-4752-82a0-40b5c39ea5bf","id":"5a73fea9-8bab-4752-82a0-40b5c39ea5bf","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T18:00:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/9ce2bcab-1f67-44cb-a417-e077741fe709","id":"9ce2bcab-1f67-44cb-a417-e077741fe709","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T16:50:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/4bd2aae4-e00b-4374-9155-42f16d32708b","id":"4bd2aae4-e00b-4374-9155-42f16d32708b","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T15:13:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/371d6dbb-b183-4d97-aab0-4e3f71cd99fb","id":"371d6dbb-b183-4d97-aab0-4e3f71cd99fb","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T13:35:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/1a918df3-7abf-4ffa-8c1b-74a32c5244e9","id":"1a918df3-7abf-4ffa-8c1b-74a32c5244e9","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T10:46:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/c0af1a65-3da4-47ad-b95d-55bf94c65e30","id":"c0af1a65-3da4-47ad-b95d-55bf94c65e30","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T08:07:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"}]},"product":{"@context":{"@version":"1.1","@vocab":"https://api.weather.gov/ontology#"},"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/c44edc84-21d0-4a58-a7cb-d10066eb0725","id":"c44edc84-21d0-4a58-a7cb-d10066eb0725","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KRIW","issuanceTime":"2026-11-05T04:31:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion","productText":"\n000\nFXUS65 KRIW 050431\nAFDRIW\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Riverton WY\n931 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- High clouds continue to push through from the south and east\n  with the upper level low over Colorado making its slow\n  eastward progression overnight. Isolated (<10%) showers \n  across Sweetwater County but rest of CWA will be dry with \n  seasonable overnight low temperatures. \n\n- Another weak shortwave pushes in from the Southwest with\n  isolated showers (<10%) west of the Divide, mainly higher\n  elevations Thursday afternoon. Overall, most will be dry with\n  seasonable temperatures continuing. \n\n- Overall dry conditions and light winds with seasonable\n  temperatures continue through the rest of the week. Above\n  average temperatures expected starting Sunday into the middle\n  of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.UPDATE...\nIssued at 1139 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA quick-moving low is traversing eastwards across Utah and Colorado \ntoday. Clouds associated with this system are seen on current \nsatellite over the southern half of Wyoming, as high pressure to the \nnorth keeps moisture suppressed. Some very light returns are seen on \nradar from Afton to Rock Springs, with little to no impacts. This \nsystem is the extent of weather for the next 24 hours, as otherwise \ndry and seasonal conditions are in place.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nIssued at 121 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nConditions remain quiet this morning, as high clouds have moved over \nareas west of the Divide in the last several hours since Tuesday \nevening. These are due to a closed low pressure system that is \nmoving over northern UT currently and will continue to track \neastward through the day today. Isolated light rain showers will be \npossible along and south of a Afton-to-Rock Springs line this \nafternoon as this system moves over CO. Little to no impacts will \noccur due to a lack of instability and any possible measurable \nprecipitation is likely to occur closer to the low center over \nsouthern Sweetwater County. Otherwise, conditions will be benign \nacross the CWA with seasonal temperatures and light winds. These \nconditions will be in place again Thursday as an area of high \npressure shifts over eastern MT/the western Dakotas. Above \nnormal temperatures will return for much of Johnson County as a \nresult.\n\nA trough will move over the region on Friday, shifting the area of \nhigh pressure over the Dakotas and Upper Midwest. No significant \nimpacts are expected with the passage of the trough, however the GFS \nis progging a low chance for rain over northern portions. A broad \nridge will then build over much of the Intermountain West and extend \nover the Northern Rockies by Saturday keeping conditions dry. \nTemperatures then trend above normal across the CWA by Sunday and \nremain in place through the middle of next week as the ridge folds \nover. Conditions also look to stay dry through this period, with a \nweak trough possibly moving over the area on Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z TAFS THROUGH 06Z FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 930 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nVFR conditions expected for the entirety of the period at all\nTAF sites. Light winds less 10kts throughout outside of a gust\nup to 18kts at CPR during peak heating of the afternoon\nThursday. Otherwise, mid level ceilings after 18-20Z west of the\nDivide with showers in the vicinity of JAC through 00Z but\nmainly hearing the higher terrain. This will cause some lower\nbut VFR ceilings and mountain obscuration along the Tetons to\nGros Ventres, as well as near the northern Winds for BPI/PNA.\nDry conditions elsewhere with no other weather elements expected\nat this time. \n\nPlease see the Aviation Weather Center and/or CWSU ZDV and ZLC for \nthe latest information on smoke, icing, and turbulence\nforecasts.\n\n&&\n\n.RIW WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nNone.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nUPDATE...Wittmann\nDISCUSSION...LaVoie\nAVIATION...Lowe\n"}}